.TH DC 1
.SH NAME
dc \- desk calculator, in Limbo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dc
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Dc
is a real, structural translation of Plan 9's own
.IR dc (1),
the reverse-Polish desk calculator, from
.BR sys/src/cmd/dc.c ,
compiled to Dis and run by
.IR emu ,
not a host binary. It lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in. This REPLACES an earlier
.B p9/cmd/dc.b
wholesale \(em that file was a bounded 64-bit fixed-point workalike
reverse-engineered against the wrong oracle (macOS's own
.BR /usr/bin/dc ,
not Plan 9's) \(em see
.B doc/plan9-port.md
in the source tree for the full history.
.PP
With no argument,
.I dc
reads its program from standard input. With one
.I file
argument, it runs that file's commands first, then keeps reading
further commands from standard input once the file runs out \(em
confirmed directly against the real binary before writing any Limbo,
not assumed. A dc program is a sequence of numbers and single-
character commands operating on a stack. Arithmetic is exact fixed-
point decimal, not floating point, at whatever precision the script
itself asks for via the scale register (see
.B k
below) \(em not an approximation with a precision ceiling, since this
port's numbers grow to whatever size a computation needs, the same way
the original's own dynamically-growing buffers do.
.PP
A number is pushed by writing it: digits (and
.BR A \(en F
as hex digits, interpreted according to the current input base \(em
see
.BR i / I
below), an optional
.B .
and more digits, with a leading
.B _
for a negative literal (a bare
.B \-
is the subtraction operator, not part of a number).
.SS Commands
.TP
.B + \- * /
Add, subtract, multiply, divide the top two values. Divide truncates
its result to the current scale register (see
.BR k ).
.TP
.B %
Modulo \(em NOT the same operation as
.B %=
would be in a C-like language: this is dc's own asymmetric pair, see
BUGS.
.TP
.B v
Square root, to the current scale register.
.TP
.B ^
Exponentiation to an integer power (positive or, with a leading
.BR _ ,
negative). The result's own scale is
.BR max(k,\ base's\ own\ scale) ,
NOT the base's scale multiplied by the exponent \(em confirmed by hand-
tracing the source before trusting it, see BUGS.
.TP
.B _
(prefix) Negate a following numeric literal.
.TP
.BR p " " f
Print the top of the stack, or the whole stack top-to-bottom
.RB ( f ).
.TP
.B P
Pop the top of stack and print it as a literal string of characters
(its own digit/byte values, not formatted as a number) with no
trailing newline.
.TP
.B d
Duplicate the top of the stack.
.TP
.B c
Clear the entire stack.
.TP
.BR z " " Z
Push the current stack depth
.RB ( z ),
or pop and push back the number of significant decimal digits in the
popped value
.RB ( Z ).
.TP
.BI s x
Pop and store into register
.IR x
(a single raw byte, or
.BI < N >
for an extended register number). Registers
.BI >= 221
can ALSO be used as arrays (see
.BR : / ; ,
below) \(em
.B s
on such a register stores a plain scalar there.
.TP
.BI S x
Push a NEW value frame onto register
.IR x Ns 's
own stack (registers hold a LIFO stack of values, not a single slot);
.BI L x
pops that frame back onto the calculator stack;
.BI l x
reads the current top frame without popping it.
.TP
.BI value\ index : x
Store
.I value
at
.I index
(0\(en2047) of array register
.IR x .
.BI index ; x
loads it back (0 if never set).
.TP
.B [ ... ]
Push a macro (a literal string of dc commands, not executed yet).
.TP
.B x
Execute the macro on top of the stack.
.TP
.BR < " " > " " =
Pop and compare the top two values; if the comparison holds, execute
the macro in the named register (read the same way
.BR s / l 's
own register argument is).
.TP
.BR i / I " " o / O
Set/query the input base
.RB ( i / I ,
default 10) or output base
.RB ( o / O ,
default 10).
.TP
.BR k / K
Set/query the scale register (number of decimal digits division,
square root, and similar keep).
.TP
.B q
Quit \(em or, inside a nested macro, unwind one level; a bare
.B Q
(preceded by a popped count) unwinds that many.
.TP
.B !
Followed by a line of text: run it as a host shell command (see BUGS
\(em the original forks
.BR /bin/rc ,
a Plan 9 program this host does not have).
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/dc.b
(translated from
.BR sys/src/cmd/dc.c ;
see its own header comment for the full derivation, every real bug
found and fixed, and every deliberate simplification)
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR dctest (1)
.SH BUGS
Binary
.B %
is dc's own real modulo (matching the sign/magnitude convention of the
division it's paired with); this is NOT the same operation a
.B %=
assignment-operator convention would suggest in a C-like language \(em
dc has no separate
.BR %= ,
so this note exists only to flag that the asymmetry other translations
in this backlog found between binary
.B %
and an assignment form (see
.IR hoc (1)'s
own BUGS) does not apply here; dc's
.B %
is simply real modulo, full stop.
.PP
.BI value\ exponent ^
result scale is
.BR max(k,\ value's\ own\ scale) ,
not
.IR value 's
scale multiplied by the exponent \(em confirmed directly:
.B "2.5 2 ^ p"
prints
.BR 6.2 ,
not
.B 6.25
(the mathematically exact
.BR 2.5\(S2 ).
Read from tracing
.B dcexp()
and the
.B ^
command by hand before trusting it, not assumed from the source's own
variable names.
.PP
A dc script that stores a plain scalar into a register numbered
.B >= 221
(one that
.B :
/
.B ;
could also address as an array), or that uses
.B :
with fewer than two values on the calculator stack, does something the
real Plan 9 binary handles by writing into memory in a way that
crashes it outright (confirmed directly \(em exit 139, SIGSEGV, on
.BR "5 :r" ) \(em
this port never crashes on either case, substituting a safe empty
array or a plain "stack empty" message instead. See
.BR dc.b 's
own header comment for the full reasoning \(em the same class of
deliberate substitution as this backlog's
.IR join (1)
port, which does the same for an out-of-range
.B \-o
field that also segfaults the real
.BR join .
.PP
The
.B !
shell-out runs the HOST's own
.B sh
(via
.IR cmd (3)),
not
.BR /bin/rc ,
which this host does not have \(em a deliberate substitution, not an
oversight.
.PP
No 9front-vs-plan9port divergence found (the two copies differ by only
78 lines, all 9front-side robustness fixes \(em nil checks, buffer-
overflow guards \(em not algorithm changes), so both were treated as
one source and plan9port's real binary used as the live oracle
throughout.
